package Libraries.Game.Graphics.Shaders.Vulkan.Vertex

/*
The SwayVertexShaderPartVulkan gives the GLSL shared by the 3D vertex
shaders and the shadow vertex shaders for the wind (Foliage plan F6): the
GPU material as MaterialVulkan writes it, and a function giving how far a
vertex of a swaying material moves. Using the same text in each shader
keeps a model and its shadow swaying together.

A material sways when its flag 128 is set. Its word 21 is the strength:
how far a point moves sideways per unit of height above its model's origin.
Its word 22 is the wind's direction about the up axis, in radians from +x
toward +z. The height is measured along the world's up axis from the
instance's origin (the translation of its transform), so a card pitched
upright sways by its top as a tree does, and the part below the origin
stays still. The movement leans with the wind and wavers with two sine
waves of time, offset by where the copy stands, so neighbors don't move as
one.

Most users will never need this class directly.

Attribute: Example
use Libraries.Game.Graphics.Shaders.Vulkan.Vertex.SwayVertexShaderPartVulkan

SwayVertexShaderPartVulkan sway
text code = sway:GetMaterialCode() + sway:GetSwayFunction()
*/
class SwayVertexShaderPartVulkan

    /*
    This action returns the GLSL for the GPU material and the Materials
    buffer reference, to come before the InstanceData struct, which then
    holds a Materials member where its material's address is (Foliage plan
    F6). The shader needs GL_EXT_buffer_reference and
    GL_EXT_scalar_block_layout.

    Attribute: Returns The GLSL text.
    */
    action GetMaterialCode returns text
        return "
// The GPU material (MaterialVulkan), as the 3D fragment shader reads it.
// The vertex shaders use its flags and its wind words (Foliage plan F6):
// flag 128 sways; swayStrength is how far a point moves sideways per unit
// of height above its model's origin; swayDirection is the wind's
// direction about the up axis, in radians from +x toward +z.
struct Material {
	uint diffuseColor;
	uint textureIndex;
	uint hasNormalMap;
	uint hasRoughMap;
	uint normalMapIndex;
	uint roughMapIndex;
	float roughness;
	float metallic;
	uint flags;
	float textureScale;
	uint emissiveColor;
	uint emissiveMapIndex;
	float emissiveStrength;
	uint occlusionMapIndex;
	float alphaCutoff;
	float normalStrength;
	float uvOffsetX;
	float uvOffsetY;
	float uvScaleX;
	float uvScaleY;
	float uvRotation;
	float swayStrength;
	float swayDirection;
	uint reserved3;
};

layout(scalar, buffer_reference) buffer Materials
{
	Material data;
};
"
    end

    /*
    This action returns the GLSL function swayOffset(materials, origin,
    worldPosition, time), which returns how far a vertex moves in the
    world: nothing unless its material sways (Foliage plan F6). It must
    come after GetMaterialCode's text.

    Attribute: Returns The GLSL text.
    */
    action GetSwayFunction returns text
        return "
// How far a vertex of a swaying material moves in the wind (Foliage plan
// F6). origin is the instance's place; worldPosition the vertex's place
// before swaying; time the shader time in seconds.
vec3 swayOffset(Materials materials, vec3 origin, vec3 worldPosition, float time)
{
	if ((materials.data.flags & 128u) == 0u)
	{
		return vec3(0.0);
	}
	float height = max(worldPosition.y - origin.y, 0.0);
	float phase = origin.x * 0.31 + origin.z * 0.23 + (worldPosition.x + worldPosition.z) * 0.05;
	float wave = 0.35 + 0.45 * sin(time * 1.7 + phase) + 0.2 * sin(time * 3.3 + phase * 1.7);
	float amount = materials.data.swayStrength * height * wave;
	float angle = materials.data.swayDirection;
	return vec3(cos(angle) * amount, 0.0, sin(angle) * amount);
}
"
    end
end
